From ed986fc76f2e091c8f94c63dc4ff4e29f4ff058d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Matt Mitchell <mattmitchell2718@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 01:41:43 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] Add window icons to title bars

Add the i3 command `title_window_icon yes|no|toggle` and
`title_window_icon padding|toggle <px>`, which shows the icon of a window
in its title bar, next to the title. Like in i3, icons are off by default,
and are typically enabled with `for_window [all] title_window_icon on`.

The icon is, in order of preference:
- the icon set by the client with xdg-toplevel-icon-v1, by name or as
  pixel buffers of different sizes and scales,
- the _NET_WM_ICON of X11 windows, fetched lazily when needed,
- the icon of the application's desktop entry, matched by sandbox app ID,
  app_id or X11 class/instance (desktop file ID, StartupWMClass, and a few
  case-insensitive fallbacks),
- the icon named after the app_id.

Named icons are looked up following the XDG icon theme specification in
the theme set with the new `title_window_icon_theme <name>` command,
falling back to hicolor and pixmaps. Lookups use GTK's icon-theme.cache
when it's up to date, and otherwise an index of the theme directories built
once, so they don't need to probe hundreds of directories. Since icons are
rendered at arbitrary sizes (the height of the title font), exact-size PNGs
are preferred, then SVGs, then larger PNGs.

PNG icons are loaded with cairo, SVG icons are rendered with librsvg (a new
optional dependency), from memory so that GIO doesn't load GVfs modules into
the compositor; only regular files are read. Icons are rendered at the scale
of the outputs they're shown on, and rendered buffers are shared by all windows
of an application. Lookup results are cached, and dropped on reload.

Images provided by clients are only read when the icon is shown, and at most 8
of them are kept, scaled down to 128px, so clients can't make sway allocate
much memory.

The icon size is advertised to clients with xdg-toplevel-icon-v1, and the
padding is reported as `window_icon_padding` in the IPC tree, as in i3.

Refs: https://github.com/swaywm/sway/issues/4882

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
